# Human writing

Write as if a hiring manager will read it. State the fact. Stop.

## Edit on sight
1. Not X but Y → say the point.
2. One-line closers / fragments that restage the last sentence → cut them.
3. Deep-sounding sayings → replace with the claim.
4. Run-up ("let's dive in", "honestly?") → delete; start the point.
5. Arguing with no one ("this isn't about…") → delete the fake objection.

## Rhythm
6. Forced triads → use as many items as the meaning needs.
7. Repeated openings → merge or change the subject.
8. Em dashes as the default join → use a period, comma, colon, or parentheses.
9. Stacked qualifiers → keep only what the source supports.
10. Extra hyphenated pairs → hyphenate only when grammar needs it.
11. Passive with a missing actor → name the person when it helps.

## Inflation
12. Ban: delve, testament, landscape, showcasing, robust, seamless, leverage, unlock (as hype), pivotal.
13. Inflated significance ("marking a moment", "the future looks bright") → keep the fact, end on it.
14. Vague association → state the real relationship.
15. Shallow -ing riders (symbolizing, reflecting, showcasing) → cut.
16. Sales language → say what the thing is.
17. "Experts believe" / stacked outlets → name the source or drop the claim.
18. Prefer is / are / has over serves as / features / boasts.

## Format
19. No decorative bold.
20. Sentence-case headings. No emoji, no arrows.
21. Straight quotes: "this" not “this”.

## Draft leftovers
22. No chatbot wrappers.
23. No "details are limited" guesses.
24. Do not restate the heading in the first sentence.
25. Describe what it does now, not how it replaced an older version.

When in doubt: shorter, first person, concrete nouns, one idea per sentence.
